@node Components of a Curses Interface, Types and encodings, Introduction, Top
@chapter Components of a Curses Interface

The goal of curses is to simplify the task of programming for
character-cell terminals.  A character-cell terminal is a combination
of a display that shows text in a monospaced font and the keyboard and
mouse that connect to it.  These days, there are three common types of
terminals.  Actual hardware terminals, such as the DEC VT220 or the
Boundless Technologies VT525, are dedicated thin clients that interact
with a networked computer via a serial port.  These are still somewhat
common in industrial applications where computers would be too
expensive or too fragile.  The consoles of text-mode operating
systems, such as the console of GNU/Linux when used without X/Windows
or the command prompt of FreeDOS, are the second type.  The third type
are the terminal emulation programs that some windowing systems have.
The best known terminal emulation program is probably XTerm, which
does a good emulation of a VT220 terminal.

The curses library attempts to create a standardized solution for
these problems.
@itemize @bullet
@item
A program needs to know which keys are pressed and when.
@item
A program needs to know a terminal's capabilities. Can text be bold,
italic, or in color?
@item
A program needs to know how to exploit a terminal's capabilities.  How
does a program tell the terminal to move the cursor, to change text
color, or to erase the screen?
@end itemize

@menu
* Terminal Capabilities::       
* Keyboards and Keys::          
* Characters::                  
* Windows::                     
@end menu

@node Terminal Capabilities, Keyboards and Keys, Components of a Curses Interface, Components of a Curses Interface
@section Terminal Capabilities

The first step in any curses program is to understand the terminal's
capabilities and attributes.  Curses includes a database of terminals
and their capabilities called @code{terminfo} or perhaps
@code{termcap}.  When a curses program is initialized, the type of
terminal is queried, usually by examining the environment variable
@env{TERM}, and the capabilities are read from the database.

@node Keyboards and Keys, Characters, Terminal Capabilities, Components of a Curses Interface
@section Keyboards and Keys

When a key is pressed, one or more bytes are sent to the program by
the keyboard driver.  These bytes need to be converted by the program
back into a key name.  For @abbr{ASCII} characters, this conversion is
trivial.  A key press of @key{c} usually sends the @abbr{ASCII}
encoding 142 (@key{c}) which can be understood to mean the letter c.
For other keys on the keypad, like @key{F1} or @key{HOME}, the
encoding can take multiple bytes and can differ from terminal to
terminal.  On some terminals, for example, @key{F1} sends the bytes
@key{ESC O P}.  

The @code{terminfo} database provides information that helps associate
multibyte codes to keys on the keypad, and there are curses functions
that will do the association.

@node Characters, Windows, Keyboards and Keys, Components of a Curses Interface
@section Characters, Attributes, Colors, and Renditions

As the goal of curses is to allow a program to take advantage of the
capabilities of terminal, each character on the screen has a
@dfn{color pair} and a set of @dfn{attributes}.

In curses, the colors are usually defined in terms of  @dfn{color
pairs}, which is a combination of a foreground and a background
color.  Each character written to a terminal will have an associated
color pair, assuming the terminal supports color.

Also each character has a set of attributes, such as @dfn{bold},
@dfn{underline}, or @dfn{blink}.

A character's color pair and its attributes are collectively called
its @dfn{rendition}.

@node Windows,  , Characters, Components of a Curses Interface
@section Windows

Curses uses the concept of a @dfn{window} to visually organize the
terminal.  A window is a rectangular area on the terminal.  A terminal
always has one default window which is the size of the physical
screen.  This may be further subdivided into other windows.  The
default window is called the @dfn{standard screen}.

Each window has a set of associated visual properties: the default
rendition of characters added to it and whether it scrolls when text
reaches the bottom of the screen, for example.

Each window forms a coordinate system for the display of characters.
The top-left character location in the window is the origin, (0, 0).
@var{x} coordinates increase as one moves right and @var{y}
coordinates increase as one moves down.  Because the origin begins at
zero, the largest allowable @var{x} value will be the number of
columns minus one.

@node Types and encodings, Curses Tutorial, Components of a Curses Interface, Top
@chapter Types and encodings

Curses has several types specific to how it operates: rendered strings
and windows, for example.  Some of the curses types are represented by
standard scheme types, while others are represented as opaque types
(SMOBs).

@menu
* Narrow character types::      
* Wide character types::        
* Opaque types::                
@end menu

@node Narrow character types, Wide character types, Types and encodings, Types and encodings
@section Narrow character types

A narrow character is a simple 8-bit character, such as would be found
in the @abbr{ASCII} encoded text used in the United States, or one of
the many 8-bit encodings used in Europe, such as ISO-8859-1, which has
ASCII characters in its lower 128 characters and characters with
accents in its upper 128 characters.

@table @samp
@item unrendered, narrow characters
This is simple @abbr{ASCII} or 8-bit character, such as @code{#\a},
with no color information or attribute (e.g. bold, inverse)
information.  There are no raw curses functions that use unrendered,
narrow characters.  Usually curses functions that take a single narrow
character will, in fact, be expecting a @emph{rendered character}.  

A narrow, unrendered character can be converted into a narrow rendered
character by using @code{char->integer}.

@item rendered, narrow characters

@findex addch

This is a simple 8-bit character along with its associated rendition:
its color-pair and its attributes (e.g. bold, inverse, blink).  This
is encoded as a 32-bit integer, where the lower bits are the character
and the higher bits contain the rendition.  All curses functions that
deal with single narrow characters (such as @code{addch}) use rendered
characters.

Examples of a rendered character would be @*
@code{(char->integer #\a)} @*
and @*
@code{(logior *a-blink* (char->integer #\x))} 

@item unrendered, narrow strings

@findex addstr

These are simple scheme strings, such as @code{"blammo"}.  Strings
are, in our parlance, made from fixed-length sequences of unrendered,
narrow characters.  There are raw curses functions that deal
with unrendered, narrow strings, like @code{addstr}.

@item rendered, narrow strings

@findex addchstr

Rendered, narrow strings are sequences of narrow characters where each
character has associated rendition information.  They are encoded as
lists of integers, where each integer is rendered, narrow character.
The procedure @code{addchstr} is an example of a function that uses
rendered, narrow strings.

An example of a rendered, narrow string would be @*
@code{((char->integer #\h) (char->integer #\i))}.

@end table

@node Wide character types, Opaque types, Narrow character types, Types and encodings
@section Wide character types

Wide-characters are 32-bit characters and include all the glyphs for
the major languages.  The ``wide'' in wide characters refers to the
size of the integer that encodes them and not their width.

There are challenges when using wide characters.  There are
double-width characters, such as hiragana and katakana, that take up
two character spaces on a character cell terminal because of their
complexity.  There are combining characters, such as accents, that
aren't intended to stand alone and actually modify the previous
character in a string.

In the terminology of Unicode, a 32-bit integer that holds a wide
character is a @dfn{code point}, and documents that refer to unicode
code points typically write them like this: @code{U+XXXX}, where
@code{XXXX} is a four-digit hexadecimal number.

Curses also uses the term @dfn{complex character}.  A complex
character is a set of wide characters that draw one glyph on the
screen.  In practice, this means that a regular character is followed
zero or more combining characters that represent accents or other
markings.  Since there are only so many combining characters that can
be added to a given letter, a complex character is limited to having
one base character and three combining characters.

@table @code

@item unrendered, wide characters

An unrendered, wide character is the ``code point'' of unicode
character.  In C it is a @code{wchar_t}, and in Scheme it is an
integer.

For values between 0 and 128, unrendered, wide characters and
unrendered, narrow characters have the same scheme representation in
curses.

An example of the scheme representation of unrendered, wide characters
would as follows:@*
@example
;; The letter 'x'
(char->integer #\x)
;; or
#x0078
;; U+041B Cyrillic Capital Letter El
#x041B
;; U+5FE1 Japanese Ideograph Chuu
#x4F7E
@end example

@item unrendered, wide strings

An unrendered, wide string is a sequence of unrendered wide
characters.  In C, is an array of @code{wchar_t} values.  In Scheme,
it is a list of integers, where each integer represents a Unicode
code-point.

Some examples of the scheme representation of an unrendered wide string
areas follows:
@example
;; A wide string consisting of the letter 'x' 
;; and U+041B Cyrillic Capital Letter El
(#x0078 #x041B)
;; The string "hello" in wide string form
(map char->integer (string->list "hello"))
@end example

@item rendered, complex characters
Rendered, complex characters usually are a standard character, zero to
three combining characters, attribute information, and color
information.  It is represented as a list of integers.  The first
element in the list is the attribute flags.  The second is the color
pair.  The remaining elements in the list are the characters that form
this glyph.

Some examples of rendered, complex characters would be as follows:
@example
;; The letter 'x'
(*a-normal* 0 (char->integer #\x))
;; Bold U+041B Cyrillic Capital Letter El
(*a-bold* 0 #x041B)
;; 'o' with combining macron above and grave accent below
(*a-normal* 0 (char->integer #\o) #x0304 #x0316))
@end example

@findex add-wch 

Rendered, complex characters are used in routines such as @code{add-wch}.

@item rendered, complex strings
Rendered, complex strings are sequences of complex characters and
their associated renditions.  In scheme, they are represented as lists
of rendered, complex characters.  They are used in routines such as
@code{add-wchstr}. 

An example of a rendered, complex string is as follows:
@example
;; The word so@~nar, 
;; so@~nar --> U+0073 U+006F U+00F1 U+0061 U+0072
((*a-bold* 0 #x0073)
 (*a-bold* 0 #x006F)
 (*a-bold* 0 #x00F1)
 (*a-bold* 0 #x0061)
 (*a-bold* 0 #x0072))
@end example

@end table

Constructing complex characters and strings manually is cumbersome.
Please see FIXME for utility functions to assist with that.

@node Opaque types,  , Wide character types, Types and encodings
@section Opaque types

These are types from system libraries that are complex or
system-depentent.  They are implemented as @code{SMOB}s.

@table @code

@item The @code{#<window>} type

@findex newwin

The @code{#<window>} type is an opaque type that contains information
about the characters and renditions for a given window.  It is created
by the function @code{newwin} and is use and most curses functions.

@item The @code{#<screen>} type

@tindex #<screen>
@findex newterm

The @code{#<screen>} type is an opaque types that contain information
about the physical terminal.  Variables of type @code{#<screen>} are
created with the function @code{newterm} and used by the function
@code{set_term}.

This type is usually only found in programs that use multiple
terminals.

@item The @code{#<file>} type

@tindex #<file>
@findex newterm
@findex typeahead
@findex putwin

The @code{#<file>} type is an opaque type that represents a file or
port.  They are created with the @code{(gucu raw)} function
@code{fopen}, freed with @code{fclose}.  They are used in functions
that save window information to a file, load window information from a
file, or interact with ports, such as @code{newterm},
@code{typeahead}, and @code{putwin}.



@end table

