@node Adding strings of characters and attributes, Adding strings, Adding characters, Curses Reference
@section Adding strings of characters and attributes

@deffn Procedure addchstr chstr
@deffnx Procedure addchnstr chstr n
@deffnx Procedure waddchstr win chstr
@deffnx Procedure waddchnstr win chstr n
@deffnx Procedure mvaddchstr y x chstr
@deffnx Procedure mvaddchnstr y x chstr n
@deffnx Procedure mvwaddchstr win y x chstr
@deffnx Procedure mvwaddchnstr win y x chstr n

These routines copy the rendered, narrow string @var{chstr} into the
window image structure at and after the current cursor position.  The
four routines with @var{n} as the last argument copy at most n
elements, but no more than will fit on the line.  If @var{n}=-1 then
the whole string is copied, to the maximum number of characters that
will fit on the line.

@findex waddnstr

The window cursor is not advanced, and these routines work faster than
@code{waddnstr}.  On the other hand, they do not perform any kind of
checking (such as for the newline, backspace, or carriage return
characters), they do not advance the current cursor position, they do
not expand other control characters to ^-escapes, and they truncate
the string if it crosses the right margin, rather than wrapping it
around to the new line.

All routines return the integer @code{*err*} if the window has already
been deleted and @code{*ok*} on success.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure add-wchstr wchstr
@deffnx Procedure add-wchnstr wchstr n
@deffnx Procedure wadd-wchstr win wchstr
@deffnx Procedure wadd-wchnstr win wchstr n
@deffnx Procedure mvadd-wchstr y x wchstr
@deffnx Procedure mvadd-wchnstr y x wchstr n
@deffnx Procedure mvwadd-wchstr win y x wchstr
@deffnx Procedure mvwadd-wchnstr win y x wchstr n

These routines copy the array of complex characters @var{wchstr} into
the window image structure at and after the current cursor position.
The four routines with @var{n} as the last argument copy at most
@var{n} elements, but no more than will fit on the line.  If
@var{n}=-1 then the whole array is copied, to the maximum number of
characters that will fit on the line.

The window cursor is not advanced.  These routines work faster than
@var{wadd-wnstr}.  On the other hand, they do not perform checking (such as
for the newline, backspace, or carriage return characters), they do
not advance the current cursor position, they do not expand other
control characters to ^-escapes, and they truncate the string if it
crosses the right margin, rather than wrapping it around to the new
line.

If a complex character cannot
completely fit at the end of the current line, the remaining columns
are filled with the background character and rendition.

All routines return the integer @code{*err*} upon failure and
@code{*ok*} on success.

@end deffn



@node Adding strings, Character and window attribute control routines, Adding strings of characters and attributes, Curses Reference
@section Adding strings 

@deffn Procecure addstr
@deffnx Procedure addnstr str n
@deffnx Procedure waddstr win str
@deffnx Procedure waddnstr win str n
@deffnx Procedure mvaddstr y x str
@deffnx Procecure mvaddnstr y x str n
@deffnx Procecure mvwaddstr win y x str
@deffnx Procecure mvwaddnstr win y x str n

@findex waddch

These routines write the characters of the unrendered, narrow string
@var{str} on the given window. It is similar to calling @code{waddch}
once for each character in the string.  The four routines with @var{n}
as the last argument write at most n characters.  If @var{n} is -1,
then the entire string will be added, up to the maximum number of
characters that will fit on the line, or until a terminating null is
reached.

All routines return the integer @code{*err*} upon failure and @code{*ok*}
on success.

@end deffn

@deffn Procecure addwstr str
@deffnx Procedure addnwstr str n
@deffnx Procedure waddwstr win str
@deffnx Procedure waddnwstr win str n
@deffnx Procedure mvaddwstr y x str
@deffnx Procecure mvaddnwstr y x str n
@deffnx Procecure mvwaddwstr win y x str
@deffnx Procecure mvwaddnwstr win y x str n

@findex waddch

These routines write the characters of the unrendered, wide string
@var{str} on the given window. It is similar to constructing a
rendered, complex character for each unrendered wide character in the
string, and then calling @code{wadd-wch} once for each resulting
rendered, complex character.  The four routines with @var{n} as the
last argument write at most n characters.  If @var{n} is -1, then the
entire string will be added, up to the maximum number of characters
that will fit on the line, or until a terminating null is reached.

All routines return the integer @code{*err*} upon failure and @code{*ok*}
on success.

@end deffn


@node Character and window attribute control routines, Bell and screen flash routines, Adding strings, Curses Reference
@section Character and window attribute control routines

@findex waddch
@findex waddstr

These routines manipulate the current attributes of the named window.
The current attributes of a window apply to all characters that are
written into the window with @code{waddch} and @code{waddstr}.
Attributes are a property of the character, and move with the
character through any scrolling and insert/delete line/character
operations.  To the extent possible, they are displayed as appropriate
modifications to the graphic rendition of characters put on the
screen.

@deffn Procedure attrset attrs
@deffnx Procedure wattrset win attrs
@deffnx Procedure attron attrs
@deffnx Procedure wattron win attrs
@deffnx Procedure attroff attrs
@deffnx Procedure wattroff win attrs
@deffnx Procedure standend
@deffnx Procedure wstandend win
@deffnx Procedure standout
@deffnx Procedure wstandout win
@deffnx Procedure attr_set attrs
@deffnx Procedure wattr_set win attrs
@deffnx Procedure attr_on attrs
@deffnx Procedure wattr_on win attrs
@deffnx Procedure attr_off attrs
@deffnx Procedure wattr_off win attrs

The routine @code{attrset} sets the current attributes of the given
window to @var{attrs}.  The routine @code{attroff} turns off the named
attributes without turning any other attributes on or off.  The
routine @code{attron} turns on the named attributes without affecting
any others.  The routine @code{standout} is the same as @code{(attron
*a-standout*)}.  The routine @code{standend} is the same as
@code{(attrset *a-normal*)} or @code{(attrset 0)}, that is, it turns off
all attributes.

@code{attr_set} is the same as @code{attrset}.  @code{attr_on} is the
same as @code{attron}, etc.q
@end deffn

@findex attrset

The @code{attrset} and related routines do not affect the attributes
used when erasing portions of the window. See FIXME for functions
which modify the attributes used for erasing and clearing.

@findex color_set
@findex wcolor_set

@deffn Procedure color_set color_pair_number opts
@deffnx Procedure wcolor_set win color_pair_number opts

The routine @code{color_set} sets the current color of the given window to
the foreground/background combination described by the
@var{color_pair_number}.  The parameter @var{opts} is reserved for
future use.  For now, set it to @code{#t}.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure attr_get
@deffnx Procedure wattr_get win
 
The routine @code{wattr_get} returns the current attribute and color
pair for the given window; @code{attr_get} returns the current
attribute and color pair for @code{stdscr}.  They are returned as a
list containing two elements.  The first element is the rendition: a
bitmap containing all the attributes and the color pair value under
256.  The second element is the color pair alone.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure chgat n attr color opts
@deffnx Procedure wchgat win n attr color opts
@deffnx Procedure mvchgat y x n attr color opts
@deffnx Procedure mvwchgat win y x n attr color opts

@findex init_pair

The routine @code{chgat} changes the attributes of a given number of
characters starting at the current cursor location of @code{stdscr}.
It does not update the cursor and does not perform wrapping.  A
character count of -1 or greater than the remaining window width means
to change attributes all the way to the end of the current line.  The
@code{wchgat} function generalizes this to any window; the
@code{mvwchgat} function does a cursor move before acting. In these
functions, the @var{color} argument is a color-pair index (as in the
first argument of @code{init_pair}, see FIXME).  The @var{opts}
argument is not presently used, but is reserved for the future (leave
it @code{#t}).

@end deffn


The following video attributes can be passed to the routines
@code{attron}, @code{attroff}, and @code{attrset}, or @code{logior}'d
with the characters passed to @code{addch}.

@defvr Constant *a-normal*
Normal display (no highlight)
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-standout*
Best highlighting mode of the terminal.
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-underline*
Underlining
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-reverse*
Reverse video
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-blink*
Blinking
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-dim*
Half bright
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-bold*
Extra bright or bold
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-protect*
Protected mode
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-invis*
Invisible or blank mode
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-altcharset*
Alternate character set
@end defvr

@defvr Constant *a-chartext*
Bit-mask to extract a character
@end defvr

@deffn Procedure COLOR_PAIR n
Returns a bit mask to apply the color pair @var{n} to a rendition.
@code{COLOR_PAIR} values can only be @code{logior}'ed with attributes
if the pair number is less than 256.  The alternate functions such as
@code{color_set} can pass a color pair value directly.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure PAIR_NUMBER attrs
Returns the pair number number associated with attribute @var{attrs}.
@end deffn

@node Bell and screen flash routines, Window background manipulation routines, Character and window attribute control routines, Curses Reference
@section Bell and screen flash routines


@deffn Procedure beep
@deffnx Procedure flash

The @code{beep} and @code{flash} routines are used to alert the
terminal user.  The routine beep sounds an audible alarm on the
terminal, if possible; otherwise it flashes the screen (visible bell).
The routine flash flashes the screen, and if that is not possible,
sounds the alert.  If neither alert is possible, nothing happens.
Nearly all terminals have an audible alert (bell or beep), but only
some can flash the screen.

These routines return @code{*ok*} if they succeed in beeping or flashing, @code{*err*}
otherwise.

@end deffn

@node Window background manipulation routines, Borders and lines, Bell and screen flash routines, Curses Reference
@section Window background manipulation routines

@deffn Procedure bkgdset ch
@deffnx Procedure wbkgdset win ch

@findex waddch

The @code{bkgdset} and @code{wbkgdset} routines manipulate the
background of the named window.  The window background consists of any
combination of attributes (i.e., rendition) and a character @var{ch}.
The attribute part of the background is combined (@code{logior}'d) with all
non-blank characters that are written into the window with @code{waddch}.
Both the character and attribute parts of the background are combined
with the blank characters.  The background becomes a property of the
character and moves with the character through any scrolling and
insert/delete line/character operations.

To the extent possible on a particular terminal, the attribute part of
the background is displayed as the graphic rendition of the character
put on the screen.

The return of these functions is undefined.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure bkgd ch
@deffnx Procedure wbkgd win ch

The @code{bkgd} and @code{wbkgd} functions set the background property
of the current or specified window and then apply this setting to
every character position in that window: The rendition of every
character on the screen is changed to the new background rendition.
Wherever the former background character appears, it is changed to the
new background character.

The integer @code{*ok*} is returned
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure getbkgd win
The @code{getbkgd} function returns the given window's current background
character/attribute pair.
@end deffn

@node Borders and lines, Clearing windows and parts of window, Window background manipulation routines, Curses Reference
@section Borders and lines


@deffn Procedure border ls rs ts bs tl tr bl br
@deffnx Procedure wborder win ls rs ts bs tl tr bl br

The @code{border} and @code{wborder} routines draw a box around the edges of a
window. Other than the window, each argument is a character with
attributes, representing a side or a corner of the border.  The
arguments are @var{ls} - left side, @var{rs} - right side, @var{ts} -
top side, @var{bs} - bottom side, @var{tl} - top left-hand corner,
@var{tr} - top right-hand corner, @var{bl} - bottom left-hand corner,
and @var{br} - bottom right-hand corner.

If  any of these arguments is zero, then the corresponding default
values are used instead.  The default values are the line drawing
characters that create a box, and they would be @var{ls} =
@code{ACS-VLINE}, @var{rs} = @code{ACS-VLINE}, @var{ts} =
@code{ACS-HLINE}, @var{bs} = @code{ACS-HLINE}, @var{tl} =
@code{ACS-ULCORNER}, @var{tr} = @code{ACS-URCORNER}, @var{bl} =
@code{ACS-LLCORNER}, @var{br} = @code{ACS-LRCORNER}.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure box win verch horch
@code{box} is a shorthand for  the  following  call:	 
@example
(wborder win verch verch horch horch 0 0 0 0)
@end example
@end deffn



@deffn Procedure hline ch n
@deffnx Procedure whline win ch n
@deffnx Procedure mvhline y x ch n
@deffnx Procedure mvwhline win y x ch n

The @code{hline} and @code{whline} functions draw a horizontal (left
to right) line using @var{ch} starting at the current cursor position
in the window.  The current cursor position is not changed.  The line
is at most @var{n} characters long, or as many as fit into the window.

The @code{mvhline} and @code{mvwhline} functions move the cursor before
drawing the line.

These functions return @code{*ok*}.

@end deffn


@deffn Procedure vline ch n
@deffnx Procedure wvline win ch n
@deffnx Procedure mvvline y x ch n
@deffnx Procedure mvwvline win y x ch n

The @code{vline} and @code{wvline} functions draw a vertical (top to
bottom) line using @code{ch} starting at the current cursor position
in the window.  The current cursor position is not changed.  The line
is at most @code{n} characters long, or as many as fit into the
window.

The @code{mvvline} and @code{mvwvline} functions move the cursor
before drawing the line.

These functions return @code{*ok*}.

@end deffn

@node Clearing windows and parts of window, Color manipulation routines, Borders and lines, Curses Reference
@section Clearing windows and parts of window

@findex wbkgdset

The following family of functions @dfn{clear} all or part of a window.
When clearing a window, the blanks created by erasure have the current
background rendition (as set by @code{wbkgdset}) merged into them.


@deffn Procedure erase
@deffnx Procedure werase win
The @code{erase} and @code{werase} routines copy blanks to every
position in the window, clearing the screen.

These functions return @code{*err*} if the window does not exist.
Otherwise they return @code{*ok*}.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure clear
@deffnx Procedure wclear win

@findex erase
@findex werase
@findex clearok
@findex wrefresh

The @code{clear} and @code{wclear} routines are like @code{erase} and
@code{werase}, but they also call @code{clearok}, so that the screen
is cleared completely on the next call to @code{wrefresh} for that window and
repainted from scratch.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure clrtobot
@deffnx Procedure wclrtobot win

The @code{clrtobot} and @code{wclrtobot} routines erase from the
cursor to the end of screen. That is, they erase all lines below the
cursor in the window.  Also, the current line to the right of the
cursor, inclusive, is erased.

These functions return @code{*err*} if the window does not exist.
Otherwise they return @code{*ok*}.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure clrtoeol
@deffnx Procedure wclrtoeol win

The @code{clrtoeol} and @code{wclrtoeol} routines erase the current line to the right
of the cursor, inclusive, to the end of the current line.

These functions return @code{*err*} if the window does not exist.
Otherwise they return @code{*ok*}.
@end deffn

@node Color manipulation routines, Deleting the character under the cursor, Clearing windows and parts of window, Curses Reference
@section Color manipulation routines

@findex start_color
@findex initscr
@findex init_pair
@findex COLOR_PAIR

GuCu supports color attributes on terminals with that capability.  To
use these routines @code{start_color} must be called, usually right
after @code{initscr}.  Colors are always used in pairs (referred to as
color-pairs).  A color-pair consists of a foreground color (for
characters) and a background color (for the blank field on which the
characters are displayed).  A programmer initializes a color-pair with
the routine @code{init_pair}.  After it has been initialized,
@code{COLOR_PAIR} can be used as a new video attribute.

@findex init_color
@findex has_colors
@findex can_change_color
@findex color_content
@findex pair_content

If a terminal is capable of redefining colors, the programmer can use
the routine @code{init_color} to change the definition of a color.
The routines @code{has_colors} and @code{can_change_color} return @code{#t}
or @code{#f}, depending on whether the terminal has color capabilities and
whether the programmer can change the colors.  The routine
@code{color_content} allows a programmer to extract the amounts of red,
green, and blue components in an initialized color.  The routine
@code{pair_content} allows a programmer to find out how a given color-pair is
currently defined.

@deffn Procedure start_color

@findex initscr
@findex COLORS
@findex COLOR_PAIRS

The @code{start_color} routine requires no arguments.  It must be called if
the programmer wants to use colors, and before any other color manipu-
lation routine is called.  It is good practice to call this routine
right after @code{initscr}. @code{start_color} initializes eight basic colors
(black, red, green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, and white), and prepares
procedures, @code{COLORS} and @code{COLOR_PAIRS} (respectively defining the
maximum number of colors and color-pairs the terminal can support).
It also restores the colors on the terminal to the values they had
when the terminal was just turned on.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure COLORS
Returns the maximum number of colors the terminal can support.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure COLOR_PAIRS
Returns the maximum number of color-pairs the terminal can support.
@end deffn


@defvr Constant COLOR_BLACK
@defvrx Constant COLOR_RED
@defvrx Constant COLOR_GREEN
@defvrx Constant COLOR_YELLOW
@defvrx Constant COLOR_BLUE
@defvrx Constant COLOR_MAGENTA
@defvrx Constant COLOR_CYAN
@defvrx Constant COLOR_WHITE

@findex init_color
These variables contain the color number of the eight default colors,
as would be used in the procedure @code{init_pair}
@end defvr


@deffn Procedure init_color color red green blue

The @code{init_color} routine changes the definition of a color.  It
takes four arguments: the number of the color to be changed followed
by three RGB values (for the amounts of @var{red}, @var{green}, and
@var{blue} components).  The value of the first argument must be
between 0 and COLORS.  (See the section FIXME for the default color
index.)  Each of the last three arguments must be a value between 0
and 1000.  When @code{init_color} is used, all occurrences of that
color on the screen immediately change to the new definition.
@end deffn


@deffn Procedure init_pair color_pair fore_color back_color

The @code{init_pair} routine changes the definition of a color-pair.
It takes three arguments: the number of the color-pair to be changed,
the fore-ground color number, and the background color number.

@findex COLOR_PAIRS

The value of the first argument must be between 1 and @code{COLOR_PAIRS-1}.

@findex COLORS

The value of the second and third arguments must be between 0 and
@code{COLORS}.  Color pair 0 is assumed to be white on black, but is
actually whatever the terminal implements before color is initialized.

If the color-pair was previously initialized, the screen is refreshed
and all occurrences of that color-pair are changed to the new definition.

@findex assume_default_colors
@findex use_default_colors

Color pair 0 is set via the @code{assume_default_colors} routine.
After @code{use_default_colors} has been called, the special color
number -1 can be used as @var{fore_color} or @var{back_color}. Color
number -1 is set to contain the default foreground or background
color for this screen, which is usually white on black.

Returns @code{*ok*} on success and @code{*err*} on failure.
@end deffn


@deffn Procedure has_colors

The @code{has_colors} routine returns @code{#t} if the terminal can
manipulate colors; otherwise, it returns @code{#f}.  This routine
facilitates writing terminal-independent programs. For example, a
programmer can use it to decide whether to use color or some other
video attribute.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure can_change_color
The @code{can_change_color} routine returns @code{#t}
if the terminal supports colors and can change their definitions;
otherwise, it returns @code{#f}.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure color_content color
@findex COLORS
The color_content routine gives programmers a way to find the
intensity of the red, green, and blue (RGB) components in a color.
Given @var{color} as its argument, it returns a list of three values
indicating the red, green, and blue components in the given color.
The value of the @var{color} argument must be between 0 and
@code{COLORS}.  The values returned are between 0 (no component) and
1000 (maximum amount of component).

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure pair_content pair

The pair_content routine allows programmers to find out what colors
color-pair @var{pair} consists of.  The value of @var{pair} must be
between 1 and COLOR_PAIRS-1.  It returns a list of two elements: the
forground color number and the background color number. The values
are between 0 and COLORS.

@end deffn

@node Deleting the character under the cursor, Deleting and inserting lines, Color manipulation routines, Curses Reference
@section Deleting the character under the cursor

@deffn Procedure delch
@deffnx Procedure wdelch win
@deffnx Procedure mvdelch y x
@deffnx Procedure mvwdelch win y x

These routines delete the character under the cursor; all characters
to the right of the cursor on the same line are moved to the left one
position and the last character on the line is filled with a blank.
The cursor position does not change (after moving to y, x, if specified).  (This does not imply use of the hardware delete character feature.)

All routines return the integer @code{*err*} upon failure and an
@code{*ok*} upon successful completion.

@end deffn

@node Deleting and inserting lines, Getting characters from the keyboard, Deleting the character under the cursor, Curses Reference
@section Deleting and inserting lines


@deffn Procedure deleteln
@deffnx Procedure wdeleteln win

The @code{deleteln} and @code{wdeleteln} routines delete the line under the cursor
in the window; all lines below the current line are moved up one line.
The bottom line of the window is cleared.  The cursor position does
not change.
Returns @code{*ok*} on success or @code{*err*} on failure.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure insdelln n
@deffnx Procedure winsdelln win n 

The @code{insdelln} and @code{winsdelln} routines, for positive
@var{n}, insert n lines into the specified window above the current
line.  The n bottom lines are lost.  For negative @var{n}, delete n
lines (starting with the one under the cursor), and move the remaining
lines up.  The bottom n lines are cleared.  The current cursor
position remains the same.
Returns @code{*ok*} on success or @code{*err*} on failure.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure insertln
@deffnx Procedure winsertln win

The @code{insertln} and @code{winsertln} routines insert a blank line above the
current line and the bottom line is lost. 
Returns @code{*ok*} on success or @code{*err*} on failure.

@end deffn


@node Getting characters from the keyboard, Receiving strings from the keyboard, Deleting and inserting lines, Curses Reference
@section Getting characters from the keyboard

@deffn Procedure getch
@deffnx Procedure wgetch win
@deffnx Procedure mvgetch y x
@deffnx Procedure mvwgetch win y x

@findex cbreak
@findex nocbreak

The @code{getch}, @code{wgetch}, @code{mvgetch} and @code{mvwgetch},
routines read a character from the window.  In no-delay mode, if no
input is waiting, the value @code{*err*} is returned.  In delay mode,
the program waits until the system passes text through to the program.
Depending on the setting of cbreak, this is after one character
(@code{cbreak} mode), or after the first newline (@code{nocbreak}
mode).  In @code{half_delay} mode, the program waits until a character
is typed or the specified timeout has been reached.

@findex noecho
@findex delch
@findex beep

Unless @code{noecho} has been set, then the character will also be
echoed into the designated window according to the following rules: If
the character is the current erase character, left arrow, or
backspace, the cursor is moved one space to the left and that screen
position is erased as if @code{delch} had been called.  If the
character value is any other *key- define, the user is alerted with a
@code{beep} call.  Otherwise the character is simply output to the
screen.

@findex keypad

If @code{keypad} is @code{#t}, and a function key is pressed, the token for that
function key is returned instead of the raw characters. Possible
function keys are defined in table FIXME.

When a character that could be the beginning of a function key is
received (which, on modern terminals, means an escape character),
curses sets a timer.  If the remainder of the sequence does not come
in within the designated time, the character is passed through;
otherwise, the function key value is returned.  For this reason, many
terminals experience a delay between the time a user presses the
escape key and the escape is returned to the program.

@findex wrefresh

If the window is not a pad, and it has been moved or modified since
the last call to @code{wrefresh}, @code{wrefresh} will be called
before another character is read.

@end deffn

@deffn Procedure ungetch ch

@findex wgetch

The ungetch routine places @var{ch} back onto the input queue to be returned
by the next call to @code{wgetch}.  There is just one input queue for all
windows.

It returns @code{*err*} if there is no more space in the buffer, or
@code{*ok*} otherwise.

@end deffn

